    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "about",
        "tabLabel": "About",
        "pages": [
          {
            "title": "Three Card Brag",
            "subtitle": "British vying classic",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "3-Card Brag is Britain's premier vying card game and a direct ancestor of modern poker. Each player receives three cards and competes for a central pot through blind and seeing bets."
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Its signature tension is the blind system: a player can bet without looking at their cards for half the cost, keeping both price pressure and psychological pressure on players who have already seen their hands."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "rules",
        "tabLabel": "Rules",
        "pages": [
          {
            "title": "Rules",
            "subtitle": "How to play",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 3,
                "text": "Objective"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Win the pot by showing the best three-card hand or by being the last player remaining after all others have folded."
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 3,
                "text": "Gameplay"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Players ante, receive three face-down cards, then bet in turn. Blind players pay the lower blind price. Seeing players pay the higher seeing price. A player may fold, call, raise, see their cards, or force a comparison according to table stakes."
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 4,
                "text": "Hand Order"
              },
              {
                "type": "list",
                "style": "ordered",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "text": "Prial: three cards of the same rank. The prial of threes is traditionally strongest."
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Running flush: three consecutive cards in the same suit."
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Run: three consecutive cards in mixed suits."
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Flush: three cards in the same suit."
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Pair: two cards of one rank."
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "High card: highest card breaks the tie."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "strategy",
        "tabLabel": "Strategy",
        "pages": [
          {
            "title": "Strategy",
            "subtitle": "Pressure and price",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Staying blind can be valuable because it keeps your betting price lower and makes your hand harder to read. The tradeoff is that you are accepting risk without information."
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Seeing early gives you better hand knowledge, but it usually doubles your required betting cost. Strong hands should press value; weak seen hands often need discipline because blind opponents can keep the pot expensive."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "scoring",
        "tabLabel": "Scoring",
        "pages": [
          {
            "title": "Scoring",
            "subtitle": "Pot resolution",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Brag does not use cumulative point scoring in a normal hand. The pot is awarded to the last remaining player or to the strongest three-card hand at comparison."
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "The ranking model is hierarchical: prial, running flush, run, flush, pair, then high card. Special table rules can make the prial of threes the highest possible hand."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "description": "3-Card Brag is Britain's classic three-card vying game. Blind and seeing betting costs create a fast contest of hand strength, pressure, and nerve.",
    "tags": [
